  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">REDUCING DEMAND FOR SAIGA HORN IN JAPAN AND SINGAPORE</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">THE CRITICALLY ENDANGERED SAIGA ANTELOPE (SAIGA SPP.) IS HUNTED FOR ITS HORN, WHICH IS USED AS AN INGREDIENT IN TRADITIONAL ASIAN MEDICINES. UNSUSTAINABLE AND ILLEGAL HUNTING HAS LED TO SIGNIFICANT DECLINES IN SAIGA POPULATIONS, AND AS A RESULT, NO LEGAL INTERNATIONAL TRADE IN WILD SAIGA PRODUCTS HAS BEEN PERMITTED SINCE 2019. HOWEVER, LEGAL DOMESTIC MARKETS IN SOME COUNTRIES STILL EXIST, AND RECENT CUSTOMS SEIZURES SHOW THAT ILLEGAL TRADE IS STILL TAKING PLACE. REDUCING CONSUMER DEMAND FOR SAIGA HORN MEDICINAL PRODUCTS IS A CONSERVATION PRIORITY, BUT LITTLE IS KNOWN ABOUT CONSUMER BEHAVIOUR IN KEY IMPORTING COUNTRIES SUCH AS JAPAN, WHERE THE HORN IS USED IN SEVERAL OVER-THE-COUNTER MEDICINES. ANOTHER MAJOR CONSUMER COUNTRY, SINGAPORE, HAS BEEN THE FOCUS OF RECENT SAIGA HORN CONSUMER STUDIES, WHICH UNDERPINNED WORK TO REDUCE DEMAND AMONGST A KEY CONSUMER GROUP. WHILE THIS SHOWED PROMISING RESULTS, FURTHER WORK IN SINGAPORE IS NEEDED TO UNDERSTAND HOW SAIGA HORN CONSUMPTION MAY HAVE CHANGED SINCE THE COVID-19 PANDEMIC, AND HOW BEHAVIOUR CHANGE WORK CAN BE EXPANDED TO HAVE GREATER IMPACT. IN THIS PROJECT WE AIM TO REDUCE DEMAND FOR SAIGA HORN PRODUCTS IN JAPAN AND SINGAPORE BY FIRST COLLECTING INFORMATION ABOUT CONSUMER BEHAVIOUR IN BOTH COUNTRIES TO IDENTIFY THE MAIN DRIVERS OF CONSUMPTION. WE WILL THEN USE THIS</narrative>
  </description>
